# Console Logging Guidelines

## Summary

This document provides comprehensive guidelines for console logging in the application. It covers:

- **General Rules**: Never leave console.log in production, never log sensitive information, use appropriate log levels
- **Debugging Logs**: When and how to use console logs for temporary debugging
- **Preventing Duplicate Logs**: Critical patterns to prevent duplicate logs in React components (data attributes, useRef, flags, debouncing)
- **Log Format Standards**: Consistent prefixes, structured data, and flattened log objects for easier debugging
- **Script Management**: Reusing existing scripts instead of creating new ones for each test
- **Cleanup Before Committing**: Pre-commit checklist for removing development logs

## 🚫 General Rules

- ❌ **NEVER leave console.log statements in production code** - Remove all development logs before committing
- ❌ **NEVER log sensitive information** - No passwords, tokens, API keys, or personal data
- ✅ **Use appropriate log levels** - `console.error` for errors, `console.warn` for warnings, `console.log` only for development debugging
- ✅ **Remove logs after debugging** - Logs are temporary tools, not permanent code

## 🔍 Debugging Logs

### When to Use Console Logs

- ✅ **Temporary debugging** - When investigating a specific issue
- ✅ **Development only** - Never commit logs to production
- ✅ **Structured data** - Log objects/arrays for better readability
- ❌ **Avoid in production code** - Use proper error handling and logging services instead

### Best Practices

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD: Structured logging with context
console.log('[ComponentName] Action performed:', {
    userId: user.id,
    action: 'update',
    timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
});

// ✅ GOOD: Conditional logging (development only)
if (process.env.NODE_ENV === 'development') {
    console.log('[ComponentName] Debug info:', data);
}

// ❌ BAD: Unstructured logs
console.log('data:', data, 'user:', user, 'status:', status);

// ❌ BAD: Logging in production code
console.log('User updated:', user); // Remove before commit
```

## 🚫 Preventing Duplicate Logs

### Critical Requirement: No Duplicate Logs

**ALWAYS prevent duplicate console logs**, especially in React components that may render multiple times due to:
- React Strict Mode (double rendering in development)
- Component re-renders
- useEffect/useLayoutEffect multiple executions
- Ref callbacks being called multiple times

### Standard Patterns

#### Pattern 1: Use Data Attributes to Track Logged State

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD: Prevent duplicate logs using data attributes
ref={(el: HTMLDivElement | null) => {
    if (el && !el.hasAttribute('data-logged')) {
        el.setAttribute('data-logged', 'true');
        console.log('[Component] One-time log:', data);
    }
}}
```

#### Pattern 2: Use useRef to Track Logged State

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD: Use ref to prevent duplicate logs
const hasLoggedRef = useRef(false);

useEffect(() => {
    if (!hasLoggedRef.current) {
        hasLoggedRef.current = true;
        console.log('[Component] One-time log:', data);
    }
}, [data]);
```

#### Pattern 3: Use Flags for Conditional Logging

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD: Use a flag to prevent duplicate logs
const [hasLogged, setHasLogged] = useState(false);

useEffect(() => {
    if (!hasLogged && condition) {
        setHasLogged(true);
        console.log('[Component] One-time log:', data);
    }
}, [condition, hasLogged]);
```

#### Pattern 4: Debounce Logging for Frequent Events

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD: Debounce logs for frequent events (resize, scroll, etc.)
const debouncedLog = useMemo(
    () => debounce((data: any) => {
        console.log('[Component] Debounced log:', data);
    }, 300),
    []
);

useEffect(() => {
    debouncedLog(data);
    return () => debouncedLog.cancel();
}, [data, debouncedLog]);
```

### Anti-Patterns to Avoid

```typescript
// ❌ BAD: Logging directly in render (will log on every render)
const MyComponent = () => {
    console.log('[Component] Rendering'); // Logs multiple times!
    return <div>Content</div>;
};

// ❌ BAD: Logging in useEffect without guards (may log multiple times)
useEffect(() => {
    console.log('[Component] Effect ran'); // May log multiple times in Strict Mode
}, [dependency]);

// ❌ BAD: Logging in ref callback without guards
ref={(el) => {
    console.log('[Component] Ref callback'); // May be called multiple times
}}

// ❌ BAD: Logging in loops without deduplication
items.forEach((item) => {
    console.log('[Component] Item:', item); // Logs for every item, every render
});
```

## 📊 Logging in React Components

### Component Lifecycle Logging

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD: One-time mount logging with guard
const MyComponent: React.FC = () => {
    const hasLoggedMountRef = useRef(false);
    
    useEffect(() => {
        if (!hasLoggedMountRef.current) {
            hasLoggedMountRef.current = true;
            console.log('[MyComponent] Mounted');
        }
    }, []);

    return <div>Content</div>;
};
```

### Event Handler Logging

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD: Log events with context, but avoid excessive logging
const handleClick = useCallback((id: number) => {
    // Only log if needed for debugging
    if (process.env.NODE_ENV === 'development') {
        console.log('[MyComponent] Click:', { id, timestamp: Date.now() });
    }
    // Handle click...
}, []);
```

### Virtual Scroll / Infinite Scroll Logging

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD: Log only for specific items, with deduplication
const renderItem = useCallback((item: any, index: number) => {
    const itemRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);
    
    return (
        <div
            ref={(el) => {
                if (el && index < 3 && !el.hasAttribute('data-logged')) {
                    el.setAttribute('data-logged', 'true');
                    console.log(`[VirtualList] Item ${index}:`, item);
                }
            }}
        >
            {item.content}
        </div>
    );
}, []);
```

## 🧹 Cleanup Before Committing

### Pre-Commit Checklist

1. ✅ **Remove all console.log statements** - Use search to find all instances
2. ✅ **Keep only console.error for critical failures** - These should remain
3. ✅ **Remove debug logs from useEffect/useLayoutEffect** - Clean up temporary debugging
4. ✅ **Remove logs from ref callbacks** - Unless they're permanent error handlers
5. ✅ **Remove conditional development logs** - Even `if (process.env.NODE_ENV === 'development')` should be removed

### Finding and Removing Logs

```bash
# Find all console.log statements
grep -r "console.log" --include="*.ts" --include="*.tsx" .

# Find console.log in specific directory
grep -r "console.log" shared/layout-components/grid/
```

## 📝 Log Format Standards

### Consistent Prefixes

Use consistent prefixes for easier filtering in console:

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD: Consistent prefix pattern
console.log('[ComponentName] Action:', data);
console.log('[ComponentName] State change:', state);
console.error('[ComponentName] Error:', error);

// ❌ BAD: Inconsistent or no prefixes
console.log('Action:', data);
console.log('ComponentName - State:', state);
```

### Structured Data

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD: Log structured objects
console.log('[ComponentName] User updated:', {
    userId: user.id,
    changes: diff,
    timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
});

// ❌ BAD: Multiple separate logs
console.log('[ComponentName] User ID:', user.id);
console.log('[ComponentName] Changes:', diff);
console.log('[ComponentName] Timestamp:', new Date());
```

### Flatten Log Objects (Critical for Debugging)

**ALWAYS flatten log objects** - Put all important information at the top level so you don't need to drill down in the console.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD: Nested structure requires drilling down
console.log('[DataGridRow] Row 2 background check:', {
    rowBackgroundColor: computed.backgroundColor,
    calculatedBgColor,
    match: computed.backgroundColor === calculatedBgColor,
    cellBackgrounds: [
        { field: 'checkbox', bg: 'rgba(0, 0, 0, 0)', isTransparent: true },
        { field: '__rowNumber', bg: 'rgba(0, 0, 0, 0)', isTransparent: true },
        // ... more cells
    ],
    hasNonTransparentCells: false,
    parentBackground: parentBg,
});

// ✅ GOOD: Flattened structure - all key info at top level
console.log('[DataGridRow] Row 2 background check:', {
    // Key metrics at top level
    rowBackgroundColor: computed.backgroundColor,
    calculatedBgColor,
    match: computed.backgroundColor === calculatedBgColor,
    hasNonTransparentCells: false,
    parentBackground: parentBg,
    // Flatten cell data - use descriptive keys
    cell0_field: 'checkbox',
    cell0_bg: 'rgba(0, 0, 0, 0)',
    cell0_isTransparent: true,
    cell1_field: '__rowNumber',
    cell1_bg: 'rgba(0, 0, 0, 0)',
    cell1_isTransparent: true,
    cell2_field: 'Customer.name',
    cell2_bg: 'rgba(0, 0, 0, 0)',
    cell2_isTransparent: true,
    // Or use a summary for arrays
    cellCount: cellBackgrounds.length,
    allCellsTransparent: cellBackgrounds.every(c => c.isTransparent),
    problematicCells: cellBackgrounds.filter(c => !c.isTransparent).map(c => c.field),
});

// ✅ GOOD: For complex data, flatten key properties and summarize arrays
console.log('[Component] State update:', {
    // Flatten key properties
    userId: user.id,
    userName: user.name,
    userEmail: user.email,
    // Summarize arrays/objects
    permissionsCount: user.permissions.length,
    hasAdminPermission: user.permissions.includes('admin'),
    lastLoginDate: user.lastLogin?.toISOString() || 'never',
    // Only include nested data if absolutely necessary
    // fullPermissions: user.permissions, // Only if needed for debugging
});

// ✅ GOOD: Use descriptive flattened keys for nested data
console.log('[API] Request failed:', {
    // Flatten error details
    errorMessage: error.message,
    errorCode: error.code,
    errorStatus: error.status,
    // Flatten request details
    requestUrl: request.url,
    requestMethod: request.method,
    requestBody: JSON.stringify(request.body),
    // Flatten response details if available
    responseStatus: response?.status,
    responseData: response?.data ? JSON.stringify(response.data) : null,
});

// ❌ BAD: Deep nesting makes debugging harder
console.log('[API] Request failed:', {
    error: {
        message: error.message,
        code: error.code,
        status: error.status,
    },
    request: {
        url: request.url,
        method: request.method,
        body: request.body,
    },
    response: {
        status: response?.status,
        data: response?.data,
    },
});
```

### Flattening Guidelines

1. **Put critical information at the top level** - The most important values should be immediately visible
2. **Use descriptive flattened keys** - Instead of `cellBackgrounds[0].bg`, use `cell0_bg` or `firstCell_bg`
3. **Summarize arrays** - Instead of logging entire arrays, log counts, summaries, or only problematic items
4. **Flatten nested objects** - Extract important nested properties to the top level with descriptive names
5. **Use prefixes for related data** - Group related flattened properties with prefixes (e.g., `cell0_`, `cell1_`, `error_`, `request_`)

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD: Flattened with prefixes for grouping
console.log('[Grid] Alignment check:', {
    // Row info
    rowIndex: 1,
    rowBg: computed.backgroundColor,
    rowBgMatch: computed.backgroundColor === calculatedBgColor,
    // Cell info - flattened with prefixes
    cell0_field: 'checkbox',
    cell0_bg: cellBackgrounds[0].bg,
    cell0_transparent: cellBackgrounds[0].isTransparent,
    cell1_field: '__rowNumber',
    cell1_bg: cellBackgrounds[1].bg,
    cell1_transparent: cellBackgrounds[1].isTransparent,
    // Summary
    totalCells: cellBackgrounds.length,
    transparentCells: cellBackgrounds.filter(c => c.isTransparent).length,
});
```

## 🔧 Script Management for Debugging

### Reuse Existing Scripts

When debugging a specific issue, **ALWAYS update the existing script** instead of creating new scripts for every test.

#### ✅ GOOD: Update Existing Script

```bash
# If a script already exists for the issue (e.g., find-border-issue.js)
# Update it to add new tests or fixes
node scripts/find-border-issue.js
```

#### ❌ BAD: Creating Multiple Scripts

```bash
# ❌ BAD: Don't create new scripts for each test
scripts/find-border-issue.js
scripts/test-border-fix.js
scripts/auto-fix-border-issue.js
scripts/verify-border-fix.js
# ... etc
```

### Script Evolution Pattern

1. **Start with a single script** - Create one script for the debugging task
2. **Enhance incrementally** - Add new features, tests, or fixes to the same script
3. **Version the script** - Use comments or version numbers if the script changes significantly
4. **Remove obsolete scripts** - Delete scripts that are no longer needed

### Example: Evolving a Debug Script

```javascript
// ✅ GOOD: Single script that evolves
// scripts/find-border-issue.js

// Version 1: Basic detection
async function findBorderIssue() {
    // Collect data
}

// Version 2: Add automatic fix
async function findBorderIssue() {
    // Collect data
    // Apply fix if issue found
}

// Version 3: Add verification
async function findBorderIssue() {
    // Collect data
    // Apply fix if issue found
    // Verify fix worked
}
```

### When to Create a New Script

Only create a new script if:
- The new script serves a **completely different purpose** (e.g., performance testing vs. visual debugging)
- The existing script is **production-critical** and shouldn't be modified
- The new script is for a **different component/system** entirely

### Script Naming Convention

Use descriptive, purpose-based names:
- ✅ `find-border-issue.js` - Finds and fixes border issues
- ✅ `test-column-alignment.js` - Tests column alignment
- ❌ `test1.js`, `test2.js`, `debug.js` - Too generic
- ❌ `find-border-issue-v2.js` - Use version comments instead

## 🎯 Summary

1. **Always prevent duplicate logs** - Use data attributes, refs, or flags
2. **Remove logs before committing** - Logs are temporary debugging tools
3. **Use structured logging** - Log objects with context, not multiple separate logs
4. **Flatten log objects** - Put all important information at the top level, avoid deep nesting
5. **Use appropriate log levels** - `error` for errors, `warn` for warnings, `log` only for development
6. **Never log sensitive data** - No passwords, tokens, or personal information
7. **Reuse existing scripts** - Update the same script for debugging instead of creating new ones
